1
00:00:00,078 --> 00:00:01,302
9:20 PM,

2
00:00:01,914 --> 00:00:04,271
November 26, 2008.

3
00:00:05,547 --> 00:00:08,034
Ten men step off inflatable speedboats

4
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:09,688
onto the Mumbai shoreline.

5
00:00:10,063 --> 00:00:11,274
Each has a rifle.

6
00:00:11,652 --> 00:00:12,667
Each has a phone.

7
00:00:13,842 --> 00:00:16,394
In the next ten minutes, five attacks

8
00:00:16,407 --> 00:00:17,800
begin across the city.

9
00:00:18,217 --> 00:00:20,899
Over the next sixty hours, this becomes

10
00:00:20,912 --> 00:00:23,321
the longest hostage siege India has ever

11
00:00:23,334 --> 00:00:23,881
faced.

12
00:00:25,154 --> 00:00:27,120
But here is the part that still doesn't

13
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:27,563
fit.

14
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:29,985
The men pulling the triggers weren't the

15
00:00:29,998 --> 00:00:31,105
ones in charge.

16
00:00:31,495 --> 00:00:33,618
The orders came down a phone line, from a

17
00:00:33,631 --> 00:00:36,196
room six hundred kilometers away — where

18
00:00:36,209 --> 00:00:38,253
someone was watching the siege on live

19
00:00:38,266 --> 00:00:38,943
television.

20
00:00:40,084 --> 00:00:42,649
So who was really holding Mumbai hostage?

